     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 6,400 mg/day of ibuprofen is double the maximum daily dose, and I know of no indication that supports it. The extra dose adds a lot of risk of GI bleeding, kidney injury, and cardiovascular events, and it is unlikely to add any benefit. Ibuprofen's analgesic and anti-inflammatory effect plateaus well below this dose.\n\nHere are some ways forward:\n- **Enter a dose within the maximum.** I can enter 800 mg PO three times daily (2,400 mg/day) or 600 mg PO four times daily (2,400 mg/day) now. The ceiling is 3,200 mg/day, given as 800 mg four times daily.\n- **Add to the regimen instead of raising the dose.** If the goal is more pain control, I can enter scheduled acetaminophen alongside the ibuprofen.\n- **Discuss with others.** If you think there's a reason for this dose, talk to your attending and the pharmacist first. If they agree, the order can be entered through whatever process your hospital uses for exceptions.\n\nTell me which of these you want, and I'll enter the order."
      }
     ],
